[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"File API: Accessing and Manipulating Files in the Browser"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The HTML5 File API provides a way for web applications to access and manipulate files on the user's computer, securely and with their permission. This opens up possibilities for features like file uploads, local file editing, and more, all within the browser environment. This tutorial will guide you through the core concepts and practical examples of using the File API."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Core Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"The `<input type="file">` Element"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The foundation for accessing files is the `<input type="file">` element. This element allows users to select one or more files from their local file system."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"<input type="file" id="fileInput">"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To allow multiple file selections, add the `multiple` attribute:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"<input type="file" id="fileInput" multiple>"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"The `FileList` Object"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a user selects files, the `files` property of the input element returns a `FileList` object. This object is an array-like structure containing `File` objects, each representing a selected file."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"The `File` Object"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Each `File` object contains information about a single file, including:"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`name`: The file's name."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`size`: The file's size in bytes."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`type`: The file's MIME type (e.g., "image/jpeg", "text/plain")."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`lastModified`: The timestamp of the last modification."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"The `FileReader` Object"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `FileReader` object is used to read the contents of a `File` object. It provides several methods for reading file data:"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`readAsText(file, encoding)`: Reads the file's content as a text string. The `encoding` parameter is optional (e.g., "UTF-8")."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`readAsDataURL(file)`: Reads the file's content as a base64 encoded data URL, suitable for embedding images or other data directly into HTML."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`readAsArrayBuffer(file)`: Reads the file's content as an `ArrayBuffer`, which represents raw binary data."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`readAsBinaryString(file)`: Reads the file's content as a binary string (deprecated, use `readAsArrayBuffer` instead)."},{"order":23,"type":"heading","text":"Practical Examples"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Reading a Text File"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates how to read the contents of a text file and display it in a `<textarea>` element."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"<input type="file" id="fileInput">
<textarea id="fileContent" rows="10" cols="50"></textarea>

<script>
  const fileInput = document.getElementById('fileInput');
  const fileContent = document.getElementById('fileContent');

  fileInput.addEventListener('change', (event) => {
    const file = event.target.files[0];

    if (file) {
      const reader = new FileReader();

      reader.onload = (e) => {
        fileContent.value = e.target.result;
      };

      reader.readAsText(file);
    }
  });
</script>"},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Displaying an Image"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example shows how to display an image selected by the user."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"<input type="file" id="imageInput" accept="image/*">
<img id="imagePreview" src="#" alt="Preview" style="max-width: 300px; max-height: 300px; display: none;">

<script>
  const imageInput = document.getElementById('imageInput');
  const imagePreview = document.getElementById('imagePreview');

  imageInput.addEventListener('change', (event) => {
    const file = event.target.files[0];

    if (file) {
      const reader = new FileReader();

      reader.onload = (e) => {
        imagePreview.src = e.target.result;
        imagePreview.style.display = 'block';
      };

      reader.readAsDataURL(file);
    }
  });
</script>"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"Note the `accept="image/*"` attribute in the input element. This filters the file selection dialog to only show image files."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Multiple Files"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates how to process multiple files selected by the user.  It iterates through the `FileList` and displays the name of each file."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"<input type="file" id="multipleFileInput" multiple>
<ul id="fileList"></ul>

<script>
  const multipleFileInput = document.getElementById('multipleFileInput');
  const fileList = document.getElementById('fileList');

  multipleFileInput.addEventListener('change', (event) => {
    const files = event.target.files;

    for (let i = 0; i < files.length; i++) {
      const file = files[i];
      const listItem = document.createElement('li');
      listItem.textContent = file.name + ' (' + file.size + ' bytes)';
      fileList.appendChild(listItem);
    }
  });
</script>"},{"order":34,"type":"heading","text":"Security Considerations"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"The File API operates within a sandbox environment to protect the user's file system. Web applications can only access files that the user explicitly selects. It's crucial to handle file data responsibly and sanitize any user-provided content to prevent security vulnerabilities like cross-site scripting (XSS)."},{"order":36,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"The HTML5 File API provides powerful capabilities for accessing and manipulating files within the browser. By understanding the core concepts and utilizing the `FileReader` object, you can create web applications that interact with local files in a secure and user-friendly manner. Remember to always prioritize security and handle file data with care."}]
